This data set contains, and is derived from, the following sources. Anyone
redistributing it, whole or in part, credits them as below.


1. Bundeslebensmittelschlüssel (BLS) 4.0
   Max Rubner-Institut (MRI), Bundesforschungsinstitut für Ernährung und
   Lebensmittel, release 2025. https://blsdb.de
   Licence: CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

   Changes: filtered to the food groups used in cooking (Data/aisles.yaml),
   reduced to 16 nutrient fields per 100 g, and linked to kitchen words,
   spellings, varieties, measures and aisles. The nutrient values themselves
   are unchanged. They ship in bls.json; the catalog refers to them by SBLS code.


2. Table de composition nutritionnelle des aliments Ciqual 2020
   Anses (Agence nationale de sécurité sanitaire de l'alimentation, de
   l'environnement et du travail), France. https://ciqual.anses.fr
   Licence: Licence Ouverte / Open Licence 2.0 (Etalab), which is compatible
   with CC BY 4.0.

   Used for single rows the BLS does not have, copied by hand with their
   Ciqual number and name as published. Each row names its source in its
   `source` field (community.json).


3. Nutrition declarations of retail products
   Where no database lists a food, a row may be read from a product's
   nutrition declaration. A declaration is a statement of fact and carries no
   licence. Each such row names the product and its EAN in its `source` field,
   so it can be checked at the shelf.


Every inline row in Data/ingredients/ names its own source, and the app shows
it under the ingredient as „Quelle: …“. The sources screen shows the entries of
Data/sources.yaml.
